WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.300
Maddy: One of the responsibilities you
have as a team leader is ensuring your

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.810
team is an equal and diverse environment.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.069
Phillip: Respecting one another’s differences
and recognising that everyone deserves to be

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:17.180
treated with dignity and respect, is how an
organization keeps its culture vibrant and

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:22.990
healthy, as well meeting the legal requirements,
legislated by the Equality Act 2010.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:28.980
It is against the law to treat people different
because of protected characteristics, so

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.420
as a team leader you need to be alert and
sensitive to this to protect your team.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.269
Maddy: Protected characteristics
defined in law are:

00:00:38.719 --> 00:00:39.259
• Age

00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:40.810
• Gender reassignment

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.739
• Marital or civil partnership status

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.559
• Pregnancy or being on maternity leave

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:47.800
• Disability

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.660
• Race, colour, nationality,
or ethnic or national origin

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.370
• Religion or belief

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:55.330
• Sex

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.020
• Sexual orientation

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:01.880
Phillip: Your own organization will have
policies in place, based on the requirements

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.900
of the law, to protect equality and diversity,
so your first responsibility as a team leader

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.530
is to familiarize yourself with these polices.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.170
But you shouldn’t think of equality
and diversity as just something you

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:16.920
should be reactively protecting.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.140
As a team leader you should be
actively promoting it and trying to

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.370
expand the diversity of your team.

00:01:24.349 --> 00:01:27.509
Maddy: So, let’s look at some of
the things you can do to promote

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:29.710
equality and diversity in your team:

00:01:30.699 --> 00:01:34.750
Phillip: Take time to educate yourself
about different cultures, traditions, and

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:39.579
religions- The more you know about different
cultures, traditions, and religions, the

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.040
more you’ll be able to relate to members
of your team who come identify with them.

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:50.399
Think about doing small events to mark major
religious holidays, such as inviting members

00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:55.280
of team who belong to a particular faith or
follow a specific tradition to give a short

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.639
presentation about the holiday if they’d like.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:03.210
Maddy: Learn about different minorities
and the prejudices they face- This includes

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.859
LGBTQ+ people, people with disabilities,
and people from minority ethnic groups.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.810
By taking time to learn about their history and
the discrimination they still face, you’ll be

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.739
more sensitive to their needs, especially if
they experience discrimination in the workplace.

00:02:20.549 --> 00:02:25.430
Phillip: Look into diversity training schemes
for you and your team- There are lots of

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:29.839
different organisations, that offer training
to help you and your team, become more

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.850
aware of issues of equality and diversity.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.409
Speak to your HR department to
see if there’s training available.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.709
Some charities and advocacy groups
also offer partnership schemes,

00:02:40.949 --> 00:02:44.810
that can improve your organisation’s
reputation as a diverse employer too.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.330
Maddy: Be aware of unconscious biases-
Everyone has biases that affect

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:54.029
their beliefs and behaviours, even if
they’re not aware of them consciously.

00:02:54.509 --> 00:02:55.609
It’s part of being human.

00:02:56.219 --> 00:03:01.440
Learning to be more aware of your own biases, sets
a good example for your team and will help you

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:04.910
more effectively promote equality and diversity.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:11.230
Phillip: Make your own stance clear- There
should be no room for doubt that you’re committed

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:16.619
to equality and diversity, and that you won’t
tolerate discriminatory actions or language.

00:03:17.289 --> 00:03:23.210
You can do this by sending out messages to your
team affirming your commitment, perhaps tying it

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.540
into company values or using real-life examples.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.069
Maddy: Talk to your team- This is one
of the most important things you can do.

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:35.459
Actively engage with your team so that
they have opportunities to talk to you,

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.799
about their concerns and their ideas.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.290
This will again show your team that
you’re committed to promoting equality

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:46.320
and diversity, and it’ll help create
an atmosphere that’s more inclusive.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:51.089
Your team will know that you’re approachable
and will feel more secure because of it.

00:03:51.599 --> 00:03:56.750
Phillip: Organisations may have policies in
place regarding equality and diversity, but

00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:02.309
it is down to you as a good leader, to ensure
that the needs of all those in your team,

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.639
are met in a respectful and responsive way.

00:04:06.219 --> 00:04:10.810
It is also important that you realise,
that you have the potential to influence

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.940
others, through your own inclusive,
supportive, and respectful behaviour.

